              text: 'What colours would you choose, based on the time of the day, indoor-outdoor , emotion? 3. Would you use any specific effect like footlights or shadows, to represent certain characteristics? Example: The powerful emperor could have a bigger shadow behind him to represent the unspoken supremacy over the regional ruler. Activity 3.2 What do you think it means? In the scene given below, think of multiple possibilities of what meaning it could convey to the audience. Write your answers in your journal, but remember, there is no right or wrong answer. The activity is based entirely on your creative interpretation. Scene: Two characters are engaged in a dance/ combat in the centre of the stage with low lights, and an ancestral photo, which is framed, is highlighted with a spotlight. Activity 3.3 Designing abstract concepts When you design abstract concepts for the stage, you are learning to turn ideas into living characters. You will explore how to give form to emotions and forces like stress, hope, pollution, time, or technology. Through costume, make-up, props, movement, and use of space, you can transform invisible ideas into powerful stage presence. Design costume, props, and make-up for this character:  an AI-based robot that is concerned about the environment and intends to tell people to reduce the use of plastic;  a book that can talk, wants to tell people about the actual problems of youth and their overdependence on gadgets. 3_Ch3 Theatre.indd 3_Ch3 Theatre.indd Symbols and Metaphors on Stage Discussion circle  If a single colour could describe your mood today, which colour would it be and why?  Imagine a doorway on stage. What could it symbolise besides entering and exiting?',
