              heading: 'Part 5',
              text: 'Let us take a closer look at how music can be explored, so that we understand ourselves, our traditions, and our country better. Forms and Styles of Music Music reflects variations in geography, language, and culture. As Indians, we can take great pride in having one of the most diverse musical cultures in the world. In order to make sense of the diversity of music, we can classify it in many ways. Fig. 6.1. Participants of Kala Utsav (1) Students from Assam playing the flute and bhortāl; (2) Students from Goa singing with the accompaniment of guitar; (3) A student from Manipur playing the penā 6_Ch6 Music.indd 6_Ch6 Music.indd What is a genre? A genre (also referred to as form or vidhā ) is a category of music that has characteristic features and sounds. Śhailī refers to a style of presentation within that category. A genre is defined by the instruments it uses, the style of singing, its rhythms, and even where it is from. Identifying genres helps us classify music and understand it better. Do you know? Learning by listening By the time he was years old, the renowned musician Kumar Gandharva was known to imitate and exactly reproduce records of the legends like Abdul Karim Khan and Fayyaz Khan. At the age of 23, he fell seriously ill with tuberculosis and lost his voice. Confined to bed for years, he listened to folk songs of the village drifting through the windows, to temple bells, birds, and wind. When he finally sang again, his voice had changed, but his music was deeper, fearless, and unforgettable. By listening, he discovered a whole new world of music.',
