              heading: 'Part 1',
              text: 'Rāga and Tāla Let us recall Nāda : Musical sound Ś hruti : A sound that is heard clearly and can be recognised as distinct from another Svara : A note in music. There are seven notes in Indian music— S R G M P D N Saptak/ Sthāyī : An octave or register in music. Musicians usually perform in Mandra (lower), Madhya (middle) and Tāra (higher) saptak/ octaves. Svara Let us revisit some of the basic building blocks of melody in classical music.  There are notes ( svaras ) of music.  S and P are the invariant notes known as achala-svara/ prakṛiti-svara .  The remaining notes (R G M D N) have higher and lower variants and known as chala-svaras/ vikṛiti-svaras .  So, the total number of notes ( svara-sthānas ) comprise prakṛiti-svaras or achala-svaras (S and P), and variants of the remaining notes (R G M D N), bringing the total number of note positions to 12. In a keyboard or a harmonium, you can see the arrangement of notes in an octave, and how the same note positions repeat, across different octaves. 8_Ch8 Music.indd 8_Ch8 Music.indd Table 8.1.',
