              text: 'Chholiyā Nṛitya of Kumaon, Uttarakhand 12_Ch Dance.indd 12_Ch Dance.indd Madhurima Bārḍo Chham from Arunachal Pradesh symbolises the journey between death and rebirth and is presented as the victory of good over evil. India also has numerous harvest festival dances, such as Bihu from Assam and Hojāgirī from Tripura. Taraṅgmel of Goa is a vibrant community dance in which performers carry colourful flags as props and move to various percussion instruments such as the dhol and tāśhā. Often, the dancers rhythmically, and in unison, cry out the words ‘ho ho’, which energises them and helps maintain coordination. The Ummattāṭ from Karnataka begins with an invocation to the river Kaveri and has its origins in harvest festivals. Fig. 12.4. Bārḍo Chham from Arunachal Pradesh Fig. 12.5. Hojāgirī from Tripura Fig. 12.6. Rani Machaiah receiving the Padma Shri from the honourable President of India Droupadi Murmu Rani Machaiah was awarded the Padma Shri in March 2023 for her work in preserving and popularising the Ummattāṭ folk dance of the Koḍava community in Karnataka. Safety disclaimer: Do not imitate these dance forms without teachers’ supervision. 12_Ch Dance.indd 12_Ch Dance.indd Activity 12.1 Journalling your own dance culture  Find information on the folk, tribal, popular, classical, and contemporary dances from: • your State/ Union Territory of residence; and • any State/ Union Territory that does not share a geographic boundary with your State/ Union Territory of residence.  Organise the information and photographs you collect into your journal . Write about the different details of the dance practice, such as the instruments, songs, recurring syllables, and costumes associated with the dance, as well as the occasions for performance, venues, etc.',
