              heading: 'Part 5',
              text: 'It showcases handcrafted objects made of stone, metal, terracotta, wood, grass, palm, beads, and fabrics. Fig. 15.4. (1) A traditional potter sculpting idols for the festivals, Kumoratuli, Kolkata; (2) Animal figurines, Terracotta, Odisha; (3) Rāvaṇa, painted wood, Uttar Pradesh 1. 2. 3.  Keep food and water away from artwork, as they are susceptible to fungi and other organisms that destroy artwork. 15_Ch VA.indd 15_Ch VA.indd Let us learn about two unique traditions used to make head gears. Sumok Thyāktuk are traditional bamboo hats made by Lepchas in Sikkim. These are worn during festivals, rituals, and special occasions. The hats are made using cane and bamboo. Each hat takes a month to weave. The first step of preparation involves making the fine threads of cane and bamboo. Following this, the hat is woven in four layers, each with its own pattern and symbolic meanings. Fig. 15.5. Sumok Thyāktuk, Sikkim Basketry Brass work Wood crafts Marble inlay Bidriware Jewellery Sikkī grass crafts Terracotta crafts Wooden chessboard pieces Embroidery and textile crafts Materials in India ̓s Sculptural Craft Madhurima 15_Ch VA.indd 15_Ch VA.indd Fig. 15.6. Śholāpith, West Bengal Śholāpith is a crafts tradition practised in West Bengal and Odisha. A similar tradition practised in Tamil Nadu is called Neṭṭī . Artists of this tradition extract a milky-white spongy core ( pith ) of the ś holā plant (scientific name: Aeschynomene aspera ) to make intricately carved decorative objects. Traditionally, śholāpith was used to craft garlands, headgear for brides and grooms, as well as idols of deities, and as decorations during festivities. The craft is practised by artisans called malakars (garland-makers), although they craft much more than garlands these days.',
