              heading: 'Part 3',
              text: 'You can also have a combination of small and large parts to add variety in your scenes and narration. See these options for some ideas. Scene Scene Scene Scene Scene Scene Scene Scene Fig. 16.4. Planning of the scenes on paper Scene Scene Scene Scene Madhurima 16_Ch VA.indd 16_Ch VA.indd Step 3. Character sketches Since the same characters may repeat in more than one scene, keep your drawings simple.  Make multiple sketches and iterations of each character.  They can be based on people you know, characters from other stories, or entirely from your imagination.  Show their actions, expressions, and clothing. Step 4. Speech balloons  Write the text for each scene in short sentences.  Choose the type of speech balloon for each scene based on what kind of thought, dialogue, or sound effect it should convey. Look at these examples for some ideas. Step 5. Composition of scenes  Create two or three compositional layouts for each scene.  In each iteration, play with the size and placement of characters, setting, and speech balloons (For example, zoom into the face in some scenes, or show the body partially).  Pay attention to the proportions, focus, and balance in each scene.  Edit the text to fit inside the speech balloons or vice versa. Fig. 16.5. Types of speech balloons I am a bot I am thinking I am speaking I am shouting I am happy! I am dreaming I am angry I am whispering I am spooky 16_Ch VA.indd 16_Ch VA.indd Step 6. Arrange the sequence: Select the iterations that work best and finalise the sequence of four scenes. Step 7. Refine: Finalise the drawings.',
